# Configuration reference for pco-mcp — NOT a .env file, and not loaded by the server.
#
# The server reads two JSON files from your home directory plus a few environment
# variables. Do not put real credentials in this repository.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. ~/.mpco_credentials.json — one entry per account (secrets live ONLY here)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Each top-level key is an account name; `account=` on every tool selects one for
# that call, and pco_use_account selects one for the current chat.
# Credentials are a Planning Center Personal Access Token: appId + secret.
# `orgName` is optional and is what tools report as `org_name`.
#
# {
#   "unw": {
#     "appId": "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx",
#     "appSecret": "pco_pat_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx",
#     "orgName": "Main Campus"
#   },
#   "rs": {
#     "appId": "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy",
#     "appSecret": "pco_pat_yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy",
#     "orgName": "North Campus"
#   }
# }
#
# ~/.mpco_config.json — non-secret preferences
# Written only by pco_set_default_account(..., persist=True). A per-chat
# pco_use_account selection lives in a small non-secret runtime file under
# $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pco-mcp/ (or ~/.cache/pco-mcp/), never in this config.
#
# {
#   "defaultAccount": "rs",
#   "orgNames": { "unw": "Main Campus" }
# }
#
# org_name precedence: creds-entry "orgName"/"org_name" > config "orgNames"[account]
# > account name.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Environment variables
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# PCO_DEFAULT_ACCOUNT
#   The INSTALLATION default, chosen when a tool call omits `account` and this
#   chat has no selection. It is computed once per server process and kept as a
#   snapshot; editing this variable or ~/.mpco_config.json later does not change
#   a running process, only new chats. Wins over ~/.mpco_config.json and over
#   the first key in ~/.mpco_credentials.json.
#
#   Per-chat override: call pco_use_account("rs") in that chat. It writes only
#   a small non-secret runtime file keyed to that chat's lazy-tool process and
#   does not affect other chats. pco_use_account() clears it.
#   To change the installation default for future chats:
#     pco_set_default_account("rs", persist=True)  -> writes ~/.mpco_config.json
#   pco_set_default_account("rs") without persist changes only the current chat.
#
# Default resolution order (snapshotted per process):
#   PCO_DEFAULT_ACCOUNT
#   > ~/.mpco_config.json "defaultAccount"
#   > first key in ~/.mpco_credentials.json
#   > "env" (when env credentials are set)
#   > "unw"
#
# PCO_APP_ID + PCO_SECRET (or PCO_APP_SECRET)
#   Legacy env-only credentials, exposed as a synthetic account named "env".
#   When a credentials file exists, its named entries always win and env creds
#   are used only for `account="env"`. When no credentials file exists (or it
#   has no accounts), the env pair serves any requested account name, so
#   existing single-account setups keep working unchanged.
#
# PCO_SECRET and PCO_APP_SECRET are equivalent; PCO_SECRET is read first.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. account selection per call
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Precedence for every tool call:
#   1. explicit `account=` argument (name or "all") — always wins
#   2. this chat's selection set by pco_use_account — per-chat runtime file
#      keyed to the stable lazy-tool process; no config write
#   3. installation default snapshot (PCO_DEFAULT_ACCOUNT / config / creds order)
#
#   account omitted -> chat selection, else the installation default snapshot
#   account="rs"    -> that account; an unknown name raises
#   account="all"   -> fan out over every configured account, ignoring the chat
#                      selection; returns
#                      {"account": "all", "account_source": "all",
#                       "accounts": [...],
#                       "results": [{"account", "org_name", "data"}],
#                       "errors": [{"account", "org_name", "error"}]}
#                      Partial failures stay in `errors`; all failures raise.
#
# Single-account results carry "account_source": "explicit" | "chat" | "installation".
# Each chat has its own lazy-tool process, so selections never leak between chats
# or parallel agents; pco_account_status() shows this chat's routing and scope_id.
